# Netlify redirect rules for the LeadMatch frontend.
#
# Netlify serves these verbatim, with no build step. Keeping the API on its own
# origin (`localhost:5000` locally, the Render URL in production) is deliberate:
# it keeps the API's CORS policy meaningful instead of proxying through the same
# origin and making CORS untestable.
#
# The rewrites below only cover the SPA fallback. API calls are NOT proxied —
# they go straight to NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL, because a proxy would hide the
# cross-origin request and stop the CORS configuration from ever being
# exercised in production.

# Never rewrite requests for real files (assets, images, icons). Without this
# the fallback below would return index.html for a missing JS bundle, which
# surfaces as a blank page and a MIME-type error rather than a 404.
/*
  !/api/*    /index.html   200
  /*          /index.html   200
*/

/*
 * Security headers.
 *
 * The backend sets an httpOnly auth cookie, so `X-Frame-Options` and
 * `Referrer-Policy` matter more than usual here. `connect-src` must include the
 * API origin — the default would otherwise block every fetch to it.
 *
 * Note: `Content-Security-Policy` is intentionally not set. A strict policy
 * needs a nonce threaded through Next's rendering, which a static redirect file
 * cannot provide; a half-configured CSP is worse than none.
 */
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
*/
